<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tiradentes</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1746">1746</a>–<a href="/years/1792">1792</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Joaquim José da Silva Xavier</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tiradentes, Joaquim José da Silva Xavier, was a Brazilian militia officer whose nickname, 'tooth-puller', came from his work as a dentist. He was the leading figure of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inconfidencia-mineira">Inconfidência Mineira</a>, the independence conspiracy of 1789 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minas-gerais">Minas Gerais</a>. Alone among the conspirators he was executed, being hanged in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-janeiro">Rio de Janeiro</a> on 21 April 1792, a date now a national holiday in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tiradentes.jpg" alt="Tiradentes" width="320" height="459" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Roque Gameiro · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jos%C3%A9_da_Silva_Xavier,_o_Tiradentes.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
